  <data name="pmdorand/settings:universal.type_matchups/rebalancing/not_effective" xml:space="preserve"><value>Not Very Effective</value></data>
  <data name="pmdorand/settings:universal.type_matchups/rebalancing/super_effective" xml:space="preserve"><value>Super Effective</value></data>

  <data name="pmdorand/documentation:monster.skills/supplementary_types">
    <value>
      When enabled, non-STAB moves roll between a limited list of selected types.
      Otherwise, when disabled, non-STAB moves can be any type.
    </value>
  </data>

  <data name="pmdorand/documentation:universal.type_matchups/elements/symmetry">
    <value>
      Controls type matchup generation. When enabled, each matchup generated has its reverse created with it. This creates more identifiable patterns.
      For example, if "A vs B" is generated, then "B vs A" is filled according to the selected mode.
    </value>
  </data>
  <data name="pmdorand/documentation:universal.type_matchups/elements/symmetry=false">
    <value>
      Type matchups are completely random.
    </value>
  </data>
  <data name="pmdorand/documentation:universal.type_matchups/elements/symmetry=mirror">
    <value>
      Type matchups are identical both ways.
      If a move is super effective one way (A vs B), then it's super effective the other way (B vs A).
      If A is immune to B, then B is immune to A.
    </value>
  </data>
  <data name="pmdorand/documentation:universal.type_matchups/elements/symmetry=reciprocal">
    <value>
      Type matchups are balanced both ways.
      If a move is not very effective one way (A vs B), then it's super effective the other way (B vs A).
      If A is immune to B, then B is super effective against A.
    </value>
  </data>

  <data name="pmdorand/documentation:universal.type_matchups/elements/generate_none">
    <value>
      When enabled, the lack of a type will have its matchups randomized.
      This will greatly affect single-type Pokemon.
    </value>
  </data>
</root>